 28	// OpenCurrent opens the current git worktree with object alternates enabled.
 29	// The caller owns the returned repository and must close it.
 30	//
 31	// A worktree root that will not resolve is an error, not a reason to open the
 32	// current directory. This used to fall back to ".", which is a different
 33	// repository whenever the two disagree, and go-git disagrees with git in exactly
 34	// the cases that made the resolution fail:
 35	//
 36	//   - git exports GIT_DIR and GIT_WORK_TREE to the hooks it runs, and
 37	//     paths.WorktreeRoot honours them. OpenPath(".") cannot see them, so a hook
 38	//     running for repo A whose git lookup failed opened repo B, the unrelated
 39	//     repository it happened to be sitting in. Verified.
 40	//   - git refuses a repository whose ownership fails its safe.directory check,
 41	//     and refuses a .git it cannot parse. go-git applies neither check, so the
 42	//     fallback opened repositories the user's own git declines to touch.
 43	//
 44	// Five of the six callers write through the repository they are handed, and all
 45	// five sit behind the root pre-run, which already refuses to run when the
 46	// worktree root does not resolve — so this closes a trap rather than a live bug.
